so one doctor is gone and the next one  has come on board the time of the doctor  just happened and  honestly a bit of mixed feelings on this  one the  quick headline is did not like it as  much as day of the doctor which i  thought was  amazing most of why i don't feel that  this was an exceptionally successful  regeneration story  hinges on one thing and i'll get to that  in a minute there are a couple of  smaller things some good some bad  um i wasn't a big fan of the fact that  it made it seem like  bringing gallifrey out of whatever  little pocket universe has been tucked  away into  brings the entire time more with it kind  of like what we dealt with in the end of  time  i'm not really liking that because if  the entire war was locked away  with gallifrey in the pocket universe  then how is that any better than it  being stuck in the time lock  that the doctor supposedly saved  everybody from  maybe i misinterpreted that i mean i  suppose that maybe the real fear was  when the time lords come back  they will restart the time war with the  dogs again but it didn't feel like that  was what they were saying again maybe i  misreading it  some little things that i liked i liked  that we finally got  a resolution on why the tardis blew up  and i'm fine with the fact that it was a  tossed offline i  actually kind of like when there's one  or two little lines that kind of fix a  whole lot of problems i always like that  kind of writing anyways  i like that we kind of got an origin for  the silence as a religion which i wasn't  expecting and that was a nice bonus i  like the fact that the  actual transformation from matt smith to  peter capaldi was  so immediate and so sudden that was a  nice change of pace because  at this point we really think we know  what a regeneration looks like so when  that happened it was  whoa okay all the stuff with the  cyberman head i thought was really  clever it was a little bit like wilson  the volleyball you know from  from the tom hanks movie castaway  however ultimately it it didn't feel  quite right to me and it comes down to  the overall feel of the episode  now generally i avoid comparing russell  t davies to stephen moffat they're  running the show different ways and by  and large i prefer moffitt's approach  but i don't like to just compare and  contrast say he's better or he's better  or whatever  but in this case to really illustrate my  point i kind of need to do that  one of russell t davey's big strengths  was that he was really good at getting  you  wrapped up in the moments as they were  happening  his stories don't quite hold up to  scrutiny the way that moffitts do but at  the moment  oh boy you get really wrapped up in it  and that's really what you  need for a regeneration story the feel  of this story though wasn't that sort of  grab you and oh  take you for a ride story it was a bit  more character study  it was a bit more like vincent and the  doctor or the girl who waited it had  that feel to it now those are both  great episodes but having that feel on a  regeneration episode  you're not getting swept up in the story  of what's actually happening  and then ultimately i was left just sort  of waiting for the regeneration to  happen  now maybe on a revisit i like it a bit  better it does have that sort of almost  fairy tale kind of quality to it that i  like in general but i just think  that feel for an episode was an ill fit  for a regeneration story  so my initial impressions  a little bit up and down of course i'm  gonna miss matt smith he what he has  been my favorite up to this point i've  said that before  i am looking forward to peter capaldi  though and i'm very excited to see him  in the part so  i'm obviously still very hopeful for  what's to come and like i said i'll  probably like this episode better  when i go back and re-watch it but  initial feelings ah  not terrific sorry guys you can't win  them all  so till next time council's adjourned
 

